WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.260
You choose one of three doors.

00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:02.580
I open an empty one.

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:03.480
Should you switch?

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:05.520
The answer surprises almost everyone.

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:08.040
Behind one door is a brand-new car.

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:09.620
Behind the other two are goats.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.230
You choose Door 1.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.710
The host, who knows what's behind every
door, opens Door 3 and reveals a goat.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.710
Now only two doors remain closed.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.470
Your original Door 1 and Door 2.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:21.550
You have two choices:

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.250
stay with your original
pick or switch to Door 2.

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:25.180
Here's the twist.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:30.180
When you first picked Door 1, you had just
a one-third chance of choosing the car.

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:32.940
The other two doors together
had a two-thirds chance.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.100
The host's action doesn't change
your original probability.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:40.920
By revealing a goat, that two-thirds
chance effectively transfers to Door 2.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.360
So switching gives you a
two-thirds chance of winning,

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.380
while staying gives you only one-third.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:46.870
This is the Monty Hall Problem.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:47.710
Be honest.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:48.750
Would you have switched?

